"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nIt tragically expands the world-view established in the previous work Scythe, and delves into the depth of the philosophical questions that world held.  \n\nIt seems to show the process by which the  system, created for population control after humanity conquered death, is bound to fail.\n\nEven in a technically perfect utopia, if an omnipotent AI does not intentionally control the situation, it is inevitable that it will collapse due to inescapable human ambition, cruelty, and desire for power.  \nThis has been constantly proven in human history.  \n\nThunderhead is a benevolent god who, bound by its own principles, must watch helplessly as its beloved humanity stumbles.  \nSensing the budding corruption that threatens humanity, Thunderhead executes a subtle plan to intervene without breaking its own laws.  \n\nIt shows that the world, which seemed like a perfect utopia in the previous work, has an inherent flaw called the .  \n\nNow, the stage is perfectly set for the next series, The Toll."